'''''Begonia masoniana''''', the '''iron cross begonia''', is a [[species]] of plant in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Begoniaceae]], [[native plant|native]] to southern [[China]] and northern [[Vietnam]]. It was originally described from cultivated plants of unknown origin and was only much later rediscovered in the wild. 
==Description==
It is a [[rhizome|rhizomatous]] [[perennial plant|perennial]] [[begonia]] growing to {{convert|0.5|m|in}}, bearing large, asymmetrical, textured green leaves covered in reddish hairs, with a prominent dark brown pattern in the centre of each leaf, reminiscent of the German [[Iron Cross]]. It produces small white flowers in erect [[panicles]], but is cultivated mainly for its foliage effect. In [[temperateness|temperate]] regions it must be grown under glass, or in a completely frost-free environment. Not to be confused with the similar looking species ''[[Begonia variegata]]'', from Vietnam. Although sometimes (incorrectly) considered a [[Rex begonia]], this species is classified in the related section Coelocentrum.<ref>{{cite book |title=RHS A-Z encyclopedia of garden plants |year=2008 |publisher=Dorling Kindersley |location=United Kingdom |isbn=978-1405332965 |pages=1136}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.begonias.org/Articles/Vol43/Begonia_masoniana.htm |title=American Begonia Society - Begonia masoniana |access-date=2012-09-08 |archive-date=2015-10-29 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151029125847/http://www.begonias.org/Articles/Vol43/Begonia_masoniana.htm |url-status=dead }}</ref>

This plant has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref>{{cite web |title=RHS Plant Selector - ''Begonia masoniana'' |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/2026/Begonia-masoniana/Details |access-date=15 April 2020}}</ref>
